/**

@page documentation_exahype2_solvers_aderdg ADER-DG solvers

\tableofcontents

An ADER-DG time step predicts a space-time solution in each cell, projects it
to the faces, solves the face Riemann problems, and corrects the cell solution.

@image html documentation/exahype2/solvers/ADER-DG.png

# Data flow

The predictor uses the cell polynomial to form a space-time approximation. It
projects the terminal solution and fluxes onto each face. Once both adjacent
cell projections are available, the Riemann solver produces the interface
contribution, which the corrector backprojects into the cell.

For a linear PDE, the face contribution can be integrated in time before its
spatial backprojection. This avoids a space-time face representation in the
corrector.

# Two-sweep schedule

The standard implementation uses two traversals:

1. The primary sweep runs the predictor from a cell callback and projects its
   result onto the faces. Rank-boundary and subtree-task interfaces copy
   their face data after the sweep.
2. The secondary sweep merges partner face data before the first face touch,
   solves the Riemann problem, and corrects each cell after its adjacent face
   results are available.

Solving a face immediately after its last touch is valid only when no partner
copy still has to arrive. The two-sweep schedule therefore applies uniformly
to serial, threaded, and MPI runs.

# Adaptive interfaces

A hanging face couples cells on different levels. The adaptive schedule
interpolates the coarse representation onto the hanging face, recomputes the
predictor for adjacent fine cells where required, solves the fine face problem,
and restricts its result to the persistent coarse face.

Cells that depend on the restricted coarse-face value are corrected in a third
sweep. The adaptive implementation therefore trades one extra traversal and
repeated predictors near refinement boundaries for a consistent fine-grid
Riemann solve.

@ref peano_action_sets describes the callback ordering used by these sweeps.

*/
